=========================================
1. What is map?
=========================================
Why are they called "Associative"?
Because they associate a key with a value.

Associative containers are STL containers that store and retrieve elements using keys instead of numeric indices. 
They provide efficient lookup, insertion, and deletion based on the key. 
Ordered associative containers (map, set) are typically implemented as 
Red-Black Trees, 
while unordered associative containers (unordered_map, unordered_set) are implemented as hash tables.

std::map is an associative container that stores:
Key -> Value

Properties:
✔ Keys are unique
✔ Keys are automatically sorted
✔ Implemented using Red-Black Tree
✔ O(log n) insert, find and erase

Example:
map<int,string> mp;
mp[1] = "Pavan";
mp[2] = "Rahul";

=========================================
2. Internal Implementation
=========================================

Implemented using a Self-Balancing BST
(typically Red-Black Tree).

Benefits:

✔ Sorted order maintained
✔ O(log n) operations
✔ Balanced tree height

Interview Answer:

map is implemented using a Red-Black Tree
which keeps keys sorted and guarantees
O(log n) insert, delete and search.

